<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John Stuart Mill</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1806">1806</a>–<a href="/years/1873">1873</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John Stuart Mill was an English philosopher, economist and Liberal member of Parliament, the most influential British liberal thinker of the nineteenth century. His works <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/on-liberty">On Liberty</a> (1859), Utilitarianism and Principles of Political Economy shaped liberal thought, and in The Subjection of Women and in Parliament he argued for votes for women.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-stuart-mill.jpg" alt="John Stuart Mill" width="320" height="401" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: London Stereoscopic Company · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:John_Stuart_Mill_by_London_Stereoscopic_Company,_c1870.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Science and thought flourished: Charles Darwin’s On the Origin of Species (1859), the work of Michael Faraday and James Clerk Maxwell on electromagnetism, and the economics of David Ricardo and <b>John Stuart Mill</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-kingdom-of-great-britain-and-ireland">United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In Britain the classic statement of the case was <b>John Stuart Mill</b>’s The Subjection of Women (1869), shaped by his partnership with Harriet Taylor Mill, which argued that the legal subordination of one sex to the other was wrong in itself and a hindrance to human improvement.</blockquote><a href="/movement/womens-suffrage-movement">Women's suffrage movement · Aims</a></li><li><blockquote>From Adam Smith to <b>John Stuart Mill</b> liberals understood liberty as the absence of interference, and Mill held that the individual ought to be free to do as he wishes unless he harms others.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/liberalism">Liberalism · Core Ideas</a></li><li><blockquote>Liberal nationalists, among them Renan and <b>John Stuart Mill</b>, argue that individuals need a national identity to lead meaningful lives.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/nationalism">Nationalism · Core Ideas</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Pentonville, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avignon">Avignon</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.54,-0.1&amp;year=1849" aria-label="Pentonville on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4548 380 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of John Stuart Mill"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4998 650h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4998 650h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.54,-0.1&amp;year=1849">What was here in 1849? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
